From a8c35811992134dd6b635088e3973baeb6cd68eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Klemens Arro <1125090+kempu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 17:09:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] directory: build an OIDC directory whose identity
 provider is down, and retry discovery on use

OpenIdDirectory::new fetches the discovery document and the JWKS when
the directory is built. Upstream retries that for 30 seconds, every 3
seconds, while the provider cannot be reached or answers with an error
(0.16.22), and then gives up: open() returns an error, the directory is
recorded as a build error and stands in as an Unavailable placeholder,
and nothing ever tries again. Every Bearer token for it is then rejected
("Directory is unavailable because it failed to initialize") until a
restart, or a registry reload that rebuilds the directories while the
provider is up. The 30 seconds also hold up the server's start, and each
registry reload that rebuilds the directories, whenever the provider is
down; and a reload that records the build error is not applied at all.

The directory is now always built, after one discovery attempt and
without waiting: upstream's 30-second loop is removed rather than kept
in front of the retry below. For a provider that comes up within those
30 seconds it saved a token at most the 10-second backoff, at the price
of the whole server starting, or a reload being applied, up to 30
seconds late (longer when an attempt runs into the 30-second HTTP
timeout); for one that stays down it bought nothing. When the attempt
fails, discovery is tried again when a token arrives: at most once per
backoff period (10 s after the first failure, doubling to 30 s), by one
caller at a time. A caller that arrives while an attempt is in flight
does not wait for it. Until discovery succeeds every token presented to
the directory is rejected as an authentication error ("Identity provider
unavailable"): nothing is verified without the provider's keys, and an
error is not a failed login, so it does not count towards an address's
authentication ban. Once discovery succeeds the directory behaves as
upstream: the issuer, audience and scope checks, the userinfo fallback,
the JWKS cache and its refresh on an unknown key id are unchanged.

A failed attempt logs auth.warning with its cause and when the next one
is due; the first success after failures logs resource.download-external
(info: the only info-level auth event is auth.success). A discovery
document for another issuer (upstream's OidcError::Config, which its
startup loop does not retry) is a misconfiguration, not an outage: it is
logged as auth.error and still retried, so fixing the provider does not
need a restart here.

/api/discover answers 503 for a domain whose identity provider has not
been discovered, or whose OIDC directory could not be built at all,
instead of this server's own metadata, which would send the client to
the wrong issuer. The PACC record names the configured issuer URL, which
needs no discovery, and the issuer match that routes a token without a
domain claim to its directory (enterprise only, not built here) compares
with that URL too, so an undiscovered directory still claims its own
tokens.

The tests run a stand-in provider on 127.0.0.1 that serves a JWKS with
an RSA key made for the test (aws-lc-rs, already in the lock file, is a
new dev-dependency). One of them builds directories against a provider
that is down and one that answers 503 and fails if building takes 2
seconds; it fails with upstream's loop put back. Upstream's own OIDC
integration tests (tests/, under the Stalwart Enterprise License, not
built here) edit the discovery document through a field that no longer
exists, and call oidc_discovery_document(), now async, as a plain
function.
